# migrations/

SQL migrations for `db.migrate: versioned`. While chok.yaml says
`migrate: auto` (the default) these files are not used: the app creates
its tables from the models it passes to `db.Tables`.

main.go embeds this directory, so the binary carries its migrations.
A file is named `<14-digit version>_<name>.sql` and sits in the
directory of its dialect — `sqlite/`, `mysql/` or `postgres/` — since
the SQL differs between them:

    go tool chok migrate create create_notes   # one empty file per dialect
    # write the SQL into each file
    go tool chok migrate status                # what is applied, what is pending
    go tool chok migrate up                    # apply the pending ones

`migrate: versioned` applies the pending files when the app starts;
`migrate: off` applies nothing and refuses to start while any is
pending, so `chok migrate up` (or your own tooling) runs them first.

Migrations only move forward. Every applied file is recorded in the
`chok_migrations` table with a checksum: changing an applied file stops
the app from starting, so a fix is a new migration. On MySQL, whose DDL
commits as it goes, a file that fails halfway resumes after its last
applied statement on the next run. When a schema was repaired by hand,

    go tool chok migrate mark-applied <version>

records the migration as applied without running it.
